{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1404 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "0a8dc2efd72b88b90ae2804a28846691f6e2a52a3421901a446ae282f92671d7" :edb/db-before-t 1403 :edb/db-after-t 1404 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534716 50 #inst "2026-10-08T04:54:36.879Z" 13194139534716 true] [17592186151827 1100 "Evaluate $f(t) = 9u(t-9)$ at $t=9$, where $u$ is the unit step function." 13194139534716 false] [17592186151827 1100 "Evaluate $f(t) = 9u(t-9)$ at $t=9,$ where $u$ is the unit step function." 13194139534716 true] [17592186151827 1145 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{1, t \\ge 0,; 0, t < 0\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(t)$ at $t=9$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(9) &= 9u(9 - 9) \\\\\n&= 9u(0) \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(9)=9\n$$" 13194139534716 false] [17592186151827 1145 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(t)$ at $t=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(9) & =9u(9-9) \\\\  & =9u(0) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(9)=9.$" 13194139534716 true] [17592186151828 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/fe9901de89c17bccdaa8c0f95440a079e6537dc42fe858fd836d89eb62f29ca7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139534716 true] [17592186151828 1100 "The graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t)$, where $u$ denotes the unit step function?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2760/Source/Images/b25aad85ae1374bd6c1bd86713ad24ca.png)" 13194139534716 false] [17592186151828 1145 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{1, t \\ge 0,; 0, t < 0\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2760/Source/Images/cea5e5cfbce8e6708425a867337b8411.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t)$, we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by $2$ units to the left, giving $y=u(t+2)$.\n- Then, we scale $y=u(t+2)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=3u(t+2)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(t)=3u(t+2)\n$$" 13194139534716 false] [17592186151828 1145 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/666fe470145e36e88a33d0719268c25226606d4de10e57f1a8e97ccfa4d34eaa.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by $2$ units to the left, giving $y=u(t+2).$\n\n- Then, we scale $y=u(t+2)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=3u(t+2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=3u(t+2).$" 13194139534716 true] [17592186151829 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/74bc7efebfaa6a798b848d9f0bbc400bc84a727a933fef32069fec6a87e6ed6b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139534716 true] [17592186151829 1100 "The graph of $y = f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t)$, where $u$ denotes the unit step function?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2760/Source/Images/6058d85fd3a0bb42589d8c84a6f4f946.png)" 13194139534716 false] [17592186151829 1145 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{1, t \\ge 0,; 0, t < 0\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y = u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2760/Source/Images/63ce6d8bac7002ace80730a3619b1370.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y = u(t)$ to the given function $y = f(t)$, we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y = u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y = u(-t)$.\n- Next, we shift by one unit to the left, giving $y = u(-(t+1)) = u(-1 - t)$.\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y = 2u(-1 - t)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(t) = 2u(-1 - t)\n$$" 13194139534716 false] [17592186151829 1145 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y = u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/666fe470145e36e88a33d0719268c25226606d4de10e57f1a8e97ccfa4d34eaa.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y = u(t)$ to the given function $y = f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y = u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y = u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by one unit to the left, giving $y = u(-(t+1)) = u(-1 - t).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y = 2u(-1 - t).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t) = 2u(-1 - t).$" 13194139534716 true] [17592186152213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305165 13194139534716 true] [17592186152213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305168 13194139534716 true] [17592186152213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305171 13194139534716 true] [17592186152213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305175 13194139534716 true] [17592186152213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305178 13194139534716 true] [17592186152214 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305158 13194139534716 true] [17592186152214 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305182 13194139534716 true] [17592186152214 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305189 13194139534716 true] [17592186152214 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305196 13194139534716 true] [17592186152215 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305203 13194139534716 true] [17592186152215 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305210 13194139534716 true] [17592186152215 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305217 